+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the rest to stay contiguous
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs in order (`t1,t2,t3,…`) and resets `next` to the remaining count + 1,
+  so keys never have gaps and the next tab opened gets the next contiguous key. Closing `t2` of `t1..t4` leaves
+  `t1,t2,t3`; the old `t3` is now `t2` and the old `t4` is now `t3`. Tab order is unchanged.
+- Supersedes: §stable-tab-keys below. The maintainer explicitly overrode that decision on 2026-10-23.
+- Reason: maintainer's decision; no rationale was supplied with the override. Known cost, carried over from the
+  superseded reason: tabs to the right of the closed one change key, so React remounts them.
 - Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+
+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others (SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23 by §contiguous-tab-keys)
+- Since: 2026-09-02
+- Superseded: 2026-10-23, the maintainer explicitly overrode this decision. Kept below only to show why it existed.
+- Was the decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
+- Was forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
+- Reason (original): the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the
+  closed one and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps
+  keys" test, since replaced).
